      <workList xml:id="workDesc_a2ea4de8">
         <work xml:id="work_d1e179176">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_efb8fa05">A5:002</identifier>
            <title xml:id="_13" xml:lang="fr">Polyeukt</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_7083a99d">
               <persName role="composer" xml:id="_14">Johann Adolph Scheibe</persName>
               <persName role="author" xml:id="_15">Pierre Corneille</persName>
            </contributor>
            <creation xml:id="creation_16_0">
               <date isodate="1738" xml:id="date_cc180d67">1738</date>
               <geogName xml:id="geogName_979013cc">Hamburg,</geogName>
            </creation>
            <history xml:id="history_16_0">
               <p xml:id="idm79">The present work, which is incidental music composed for <persName xml:id="idm80" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Corneille</persName>’s drama ‘Polyeukt’ performed by the theatre troupe, Neuberin Theater, was composed around 1738. The music has not survived. This work and his 'Mithridat' (SchW A5:001), which was also incidental music, is often referred to by German intellectuals throughout the eighteenth century as being quite original. Scheibe had experimented with new ways of composing music such as overtures and entr'actes to theatre plays (dramas) based on the idea that the music should reflect the emotions and 'temperament' of the words of the drama or play. He presented his ideas to some friends and in particular to people associated with the theatre troupe and its leader <persName xml:id="idm81" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Caroline Neuber</persName> who collaborated with <persName xml:id="idm82" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Johann Christoph Gottsched</persName> on theatre aesthetics, criticising German theatre and drama. She also tried to promote French theatre drama in Germany. Neuber approved of Scheibe's proposals. Scheibe started out with the music to ‘Polyeukt’ which was performed by the troupe in Hamburg, Leipzig and then Kiel, and according to Mentzel (1902) 'Polyeukt' was also performed in Frankfurt in 1745, that is, when Scheibe was Kapellmeister in Copenhagen. According to Scheibe, the Neuberin troupe lacked unfortunately a good orchestra. <rend xml:id="idm83" fontstyle="italic">Critischer Musikus</rend>, 1737–40 (no. 67; SchW C1:008), includes a detailed account of the two theatrical pieces which he wrote and a thorough explanation regarding his ideas on the music’s framework: Scheibe argues that an opening sinfonia must refer to the first act, while the sinfonias between the acts reflect the close of the preceeding act and on the beginning of the following act; the concluding sinfonia referred of course to the final act. In his <rend xml:id="idm84" fontstyle="italic">Hamburgische Dramaturgie</rend> (1767–69), <persName xml:id="idm85" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Gotthold Ephraim Lessing</persName> discusses the issue though not agreeing entirely with Scheibe since the music will anticipate the words and effects of the play and hence weaken the effect. In November 1772, the theatre critic and editor of the <rend xml:id="idm86" fontstyle="italic">dramatiske Journal</rend>, <persName xml:id="idm87" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Peder Rosenstand-Goiske</persName>, refers in a review the reader to notice Scheibe's dissertation on sinfonias for plays of various genres. Other composers such as <persName xml:id="idm88" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Johann Gotthelf Scheinpflug</persName> and <persName xml:id="idm89" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Johann Friedrich Agricola</persName> were inspired by Scheibe's ideas on incidental music, and in <rend xml:id="idm90" fontstyle="italic">Gothaer Theatercalender</rend> (1777) it is mentioned that after Scheibe's death in 1776 his reform of incidental music was continued by a theatre troupe playing in Preußberg and Esterháza. Apparently Scheibe composed incidental music (sinfonias) to several dramas in addition to 'Mithridat' and 'Polyeukt'.</p>
            </history>
